{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "The nature/anatomy of incidents captured in ticket free-text: what device/component fails, what problem occurs, and how it gets resolved.",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Classifies the incident into a semantic category derived from short_description, anchoring the 'what kind of incident' facet."
    },
    {
      "name": "device_component",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Identifies the affected hardware/component, a concrete facet of the incident subject."
    },
    {
      "name": "problem_type",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Characterizes the failure mode (malfunction, connectivity, update issue), a distinct and complementary facet."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_action",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Captures how the incident was addressed, closing the problem->resolution loop and adding analytical value."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurring_pattern",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Flags whether the incident reflects a repeated issue; relevant to prioritizing what's worth analyzing, though slightly broader."
    },
    {
      "name": "urgency_signal",
      "supports_focus": false,
      "rationale": "A bare urgency boolean overlaps with the existing priority column and drifts toward generic severity tagging rather than the incident-anatomy focus."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.82,
  "structure_quality": 0.8,
  "focus_actionability": 0.85,
  "rationale": "The added columns collectively centre on a coherent, data-inferable focus: decomposing the incident's substance from free-text into component / problem / resolution facets. This is far from a grab-bag or mechanical text-length noise; four columns (category, component, problem_type, resolution_action) form a well-differentiated predictor/attribute set at a useful granularity, enabling analysis like 'which components drive which problems and resolutions.' recurring_pattern adds a legitimate prioritization angle. urgency_signal is the weakest link: a bare boolean that largely duplicates the existing priority field and slightly dilutes the focus. Overall the implied focus is clearly actionable (an analyst can pivot on component/problem/resolution and target recurring hot spots), so scores are high but not maximal due to the mild redundancy/drift of urgency_signal."
}
